# XINC1 - W35: the string-pointer setters call the placed 0x00cb0b10, not a placeholder overload. Method: docs/PATCHES.md.

@@ XINC1 src/staging/blk_00ca0000_o2.cpp 0x00cb0c10
# x86 0x00cb0c10 (ret 4, thiscall): saves this, loads the string argument into ecx, pushes this as the first stack
# word, sets eax to -1, calls 0x00cb0b10 and pops that one word; returns this. The callee reads the slot pointer at
# its first stack word (plain `ret`, the caller pops), the string in ecx, the length in eax; it compares the slot's
# word with the string, frees an old tagged string, then stores a tagged copy. So the call is the placed three-
# parameter definition (slot, string, length) with this as the slot, not a body that stores s + len. Same argument
# order as the placed overload's other caller; the placeholder overload keeps no caller.
<<<
    SelfRef ref;
    ref.p = this;
    FUN_00cb0b10(ref, s, -1);
===
    StringSlot ref = {(unsigned int*)this};
    FUN_00cb0b10(ref, (char*)s, -1);
>>>

@@ XINC1 src/staging/blk_00ca0000_o2.cpp 0x00cb0c50
# x86 0x00cb0c50 (ret 8, thiscall): eax = the length (second stack argument), pushes ecx (this) as the callee's
# stack word, ecx = the string (first stack argument, re-read after the push), calls 0x00cb0b10, pops the word.
# Same call as 0x00cb0c10 with the caller's length: (slot = this, string, length) of the placed definition.
<<<
    SelfRef ref;
    ref.p = this;
    FUN_00cb0b10(ref, apStr, len);
===
    ::StringSlot ref = {(unsigned int*)this};
    ::FUN_00cb0b10(ref, (char*)apStr, len);
>>>

@@ XINC1 src/staging/blk_00ca0000_o2.cpp 0x00cb0c30
# x86 0x00cb0c30 (ret 4, thiscall): eax = the argument (a string pointer holder), ecx = its first word with the low
# bit cleared, pushes this as the stack word, eax = -1, calls 0x00cb0b10: the placed (slot = this, string, -1) call
# with the holder's untagged string.
<<<
    CB0SelfRef self = {(CB0StringPtr*)this};
    FUN_00cb0b10(self, other.cString(), -1);
===
    StringSlot self = {(unsigned int*)this};
    FUN_00cb0b10(self, (char*)other.cString(), -1);
>>>
